RERELEASE=1
DESCRIPTION=Bare-metal / browser-lab relay chat now prints in the guest terminal (#426), and cat/type can no longer forge lab control lines from received or written files (#427). The page types each peer message at an idle command prompt as hex in 48-byte parts (`relayrecv + <from> <hex>` ... `relayrecv <from> <hex>`) so every typed line fits the guest's 128-character buffer; the kernel joins the parts (up to the relay's 512 bytes, dropping an unfinished message on a sender change, a bad part or leaving) and prints the whole message as `[Server Message, From <from>]: <text>`, broken between words at 80 columns, with only bytes 0x20-0x7E and colour-only ANSI SGR codes (everything else `?`). relayrecv requires an armed server seat (`server host|join` or the page's quiet `server seat` / `server unseat` on connect/leave) and is refused under lab isolation. The VGA console no longer shows page plumbing: the shell keeps `relayrecv`, `filerecv`, `server seat` and `server unseat` lines off the screen (serial keeps them), and the `SERVER_RELAY msg|announce` and `SERVER_FILE` hex control lines go to serial only, so a sender does not see their message repeated. The same filter backs `cat` / `type` behind a fixed `[file] ` prefix per logical line so SWITCHUSER, SERVER_*, and LAB_NETMODE in file content never match parseGuestLine. The VGA console (bare metal and the WebAssembly shell's buffer) now draws SGR colour codes 0, 1, 22, 30-37, 39, 40-47, 49, 90-97 and 100-107 as text attributes and swallows other escape sequences; relay messages and file lines that used colour end with a reset so a sender cannot recolour the receiver's prompt, and cursor-moving or other escape sequences still show as `?`. lab-core.js exports relayRecvLine; browser-lab tests and test-freestanding-shell cover parts, wrapping, colour, the sanitize and seat rules, and test_fs_vga_wrap checks the SGR attributes; README documents the round trip. docs/COLOR_SYSTEM.md records the color system: which color each server helper uses, the ANSI escape bytes and the VGA attribute bytes (hex, binary, ASCII), the SGR codes the VGA console draws, and the files involved. The image colours its own server text on the VGA screen only (serial, redirects and the lab page's parsed lines stay plain), with the hosted helpers' tags and colours (userland/shell/fl_colors.c): chat is cyan `[Server Message, From <from>]: <text>`, `[Server]` success green and warnings yellow, `[ERROR]` red; test-freestanding-shell checks the attribute bytes.

RELEASE_DATE=2026-11-18
